  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nBased on the task requirements and evaluation function, I will develop a specialized search algorithm that focuses on sequences with low autocorrelation. The evaluation function is 2*n*max(convolve(a,a))/sum(a)^2, which means we need to achieve two goals: maximize the minimum value of 'a' while minimizing sum(a) to reduce denominator effect, and minimizing the convolution peak. The previous program achieved 1.517996, but my approach will target even lower scores under constraints.\n\nThe core idea is to use a combination of global optimization and local refinement. I will initialize with bandlimited sequences, then use a multi-phase local optimization using differentiable max surrogates via projected gradient descent. Unlike previous approaches, I will incorporate two innovative changes:\n- First, represent each sequence as a weighted combination of basis functions with frequency separation\n- Then, use a dynamic adaptive simplex method that mutates weights across three orthogonal dimensions\n\nThis approach reduces the dimension of optimization by factoring the sequence space, making the search more efficient while handling the large sequence length (n=4000) appropriately.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n- Represent a sequence a(t) for t=0 to n-1 as a superposition of basis functions:\n  a(t) = w0 * u0(t) + w1 * u1(t) + w2 * u2(t) + w3 * s(t, rp)\n- Basis functions:\n  u0(t): Uniform distribution [1,1,...,1]\n  u1(t): Rising then falling sinusoid\n  u2(t): Rising then falling exponential\n  s(t, rp): Chebyshev-polynomial taper at endpoints\n- Optimization variables: Weights w = [w0, w1, w2, w3]\n- Objective: minimize_score(f, M)= (2*n*M/(f^2))\n- Constraints:\n  sum(w) >= 0.01 (after normalization)\n  Each basis contribution between 0 and 1000\n- Define gradient for Lipschitz-continuous surrogate via Fourier mode scanning\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\nInitialize parameters:\nbudget_s = max(0.4 * budget_s, 3600) # Ensure minimum runtime\nrng = default_rng(42) # Concretize seed for reproducibility\npopulation_size = 200\ncandidate_limit = int(budget_s * 2) # Conserve evaluations\n\n# 1. Global Optimization Phase\nGenerate initial population via multilevel sampling\nfactors = [0.3, 0.6, 1.0] # Orthogonal perturbations\nbasis_vectors = [\n  full_uniform(4000),\n  rising_sinusoid(4000),\n  rising_exponential(4004),\n  chebyshev_endpoint(4000, factors)\n]\n\nfor i in range(population_size):\n  weights = rng.uniform(0.1,0.9,size=4)\n  normalized_weights = weights / weights.sum()\n  candidate = []\n  for j, basis in enumerate(basis_vectors):\n      # Empirical scaling: higher factors get higher priority\n      weight = normalized_weights[j] * (1 + rng.uniform(0,0.2))\n      if j >=4: \n          candidate.append(adjust_scale(basis, weight))\n      else:\n          candidate.append(basis)\n\n  loss = evaluate_sequence(candidate)\n\n# 2. Local Optimization Phase\nKeep top candidates\nbest_score = inf\nbest_sequence = none\n\n# Adaptive simplex optimization for each candidate\nbest_seq = None\ncurr_score = inf\n\nresidual_time = budget_s - time_elapsed\nif residual_time > 300:\n    opt_time = min(300, residual_time/2)\nelse:\n    opt_time = residual_time\n\np, s_val = project_simplex(candidate)\ndef obj(weights):\n    adjusted = [full_uniform]*w0 + [rising_sinusoid]*w1 + [rising_exponential]*w2 + [chebyshev_endpoint]*w3\n    conv = np.convolve(adjusted)\n    return 2*n*conv_max/(sum(adjusted))**2\n\n# Optimized gradient descent with adaptive step sizes born from Fourier analysis\ngradient = compute_gradient(obj, weights, method=\"central\", epsilon=1e-3)\n\n# Project gradient to feasible simplex dimensions\nfeasible_weights = project_simplex(gradient)\n\n# Perform inverse time scaling for losses\nwhile opt_time > 0 and best_score not found:\n  adjust_weights( )\n  evaluate at new point\n\n# 3. Convergence checks\nif best_score < 1.5027 and best_sequence not None:\n    # Re-run polish on best candidate\n    local_optimize(start\u4ef7, end\u4ef7)\nelse:\n    # Use simplex assistant optionally for rounding improvements\n\n# 4. Formatting\nensure all values between 0 and 1000\nreturn best_sequence\n\n4. Search Choices:\n- Initial population density: 200 candidates with random weights\n- Adap\u5b9a\u65f6 simplex refinement: 2400 seconds total execution time, scheduled in phases\n- Frequency resolution: 4 basis functions with adaptive weights\n- Genetic algorithm strength: 0.3 amplitude for slower convergence\n\n5. Compute Budget:\nProjected budget use:\n- Global optimization: 1200 seconds (est. 400 * 500ms FFT calls)\n- Local optimization: 1200 seconds (more expensive gradient evaluations)\n\n6. Validation and Contract:\n- Each candidate passed through evaluate_sequence()\n- Final sequence evaluated outside any gradient-based procedures\n- Return\u683c\u5f0f: list of float values (nonnegative and not NaN)\n- Fallback: if no solution, return one-variable optimization sequence from `propose_candidate`",
